      ":question/id": "a41efe2e-217f-5d4e-8c40-c317692d2ec6",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-10574",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=3x+\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\pi /3$ | $2\\pi /3$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(-\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
