      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{20}}9 \\sin{\\left(10 x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=10 x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{20}}9 \\sin{\\left(10 x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &= {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}9\\sin(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{10}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sin(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= - \\frac{9 \\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{10}\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= 0-\\left(- \\frac{9}{10}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{9}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
