      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cos{\\left(2 \\pi x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=2 \\pi x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2 \\pi\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2 \\pi}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can rewrite the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\cos{\\left(2 \\pi x \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &= {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}1\\cos(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2 \\pi}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\cos(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{\\sin{\\left(u \\right)}}{2 \\pi}\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2 \\pi}-(0) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{2 \\pi}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
