      ":question/problem": "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{27}}\\sin{\\left(9 x + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=9 x + \\frac{\\pi}{3},$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=9\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{27}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $\\frac{\\pi}{3}$ | $\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{27}}\\sin{\\left(9 x + \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\right)}\\,\\text{d}x &= {\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}1\\sin(u)\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}}\\sin(u)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ &= - \\frac{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)}}{9}\\big|_{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{18}-\\left(- \\frac{1}{18}\\right) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
