      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=5x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{5}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi /2$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
