      ":question/problem": "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we let $u=3x,$ then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\pi /3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\pi$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"
